five years ago a little animation show  about a high school volleyball team went  on air it wasn't too long before it  became a worldwide fan favorite winning  over not just the Shonan and Sports  anime buffs but many others too that  little show haikyuu  is now three seasons and 60 episodes old  and will be back soon with a highly  anticipated fourth season Sbrocco dates  first the story of high cube inspired by  the player once called the Little Giant  in an to show yo joins karasuno high  school and it's volleyball club Hinata  is tiny for a sport that considers  height its biggest weapon but he's  determined not to let that get in the  way of his dream of emulating his idol  he meets his match in the taciturn carga  Yamato Bo who unlike him is a natural  talent and was once a rival hit sworn to  surpass teammates now but still polar  opposites the freak duo must learn to  work together not just for themselves  but because they may be their team's  only chance to get back to winning ways  the anime chronicles Kara sunos journey  from a foreign power house to gritty  underdogs and eventual giant killers  it's a simple narrative one we've seen  play out in plenty of sports anime and  other anime for that matter - so what is  it that makes haikyuu special because  special it is the anime ranked 23rd on  NHK is best 100 anime in 2017 it's been  voted one of the top anime shows  guaranteed to make you happy and the  sales of the manga have also remained  consistently high so today let's try and  unravel the appeal of haikyuu and why  you should watch the series preferably  before season 4 airs  [Music]  there's no obvious reason that makes  haikyuu stand out amongst its  counterparts when it has and this is  probably its biggest draw are subtle  ways in which it does things differently  this isn't just in its character design  and storytelling but also its humor use  of emotions and the role volleyball  plays in the show volleyball is  intrinsic to haikyuu it's not just a  passing theme to start the story off and  get back to every once in a while it's  not there simply as decoration to make  the players look cool in their uniforms  well as they make unbelievable shots one  thing I feel haikyuu does really well  compared to other sports anime is that  it actually teaches you a lot of things  about volleyball there are lessons  embedded everywhere on what constitutes  a block or a service ace on the  functions and positions of each player  on the court it gets more complicated as  you learn the technicalities behind the  many ways Spikers make contact with the  ball or how long a pinch server can  remain on court it has this cleverly  through little cartoon diagrams in the  form of dialogue between characters and  through slow-motion replays just like in  real sports programs so it never really  feels boring or convoluted if that's the  case you're probably thinking why you  should watch an anime about volleyball  when you can simply just watch the real  game on TV right wrong because while  volleyball remains at the heart of the  series it never eclipses the other more  important aspects  shut up  it's coming over  such as it's wonderfully endearing cast  of characters one of high Q's biggest  strengths is its character design no two  characters are alike not in the way they  look or even their personalities you  have the clumsy but intensely  competitive Hinata the genius car guy  armor with godly skills who sucks at  making simple conversation and the  goateed are zoom on it Asahi who looks  like a gangster but is really just a big  softy within a short span of three  seasons  haikus managed to fully flesh out the  characters each with their own distinct  quirks attitudes ambitions and motives  this doesn't just go for the boys of  karasuno but the numerous rivals friends  and mentors they meet along the way many  of these characters have their own  personal connections and equations that  have nothing to do with the others or  the story's main plot but still add to  your viewing experience some of them  have relevant backstories but those who  don't aren't any poorer for it  now I've heard many anime fans say that  a series loses out when it doesn't have  character development and progression if  this were true and indeed it makes sense  anime series with a relatively shorter  runtime are at a distinct disadvantage  over their longer running rivals in the  case of haikyuu the story told so far  makes up less than a year in the lives  of its characters short of uprooting the  story from reality and taking it into  unknown in Supernatural territory it's  impossible to expect high Q's characters  to show a great deal of development and  progression but that's not to say they  don't change and grow they do but in  small important ways perhaps the best  example of this is the transformation of  tsukiyojima you first see him as a  player who has potential but is held  back by his aloofness and his disdain of  those who try to hard call collecting  and caustic tongued he never passes up  an opportunity to dis sonata in car  gamma for being slaves to their passion  but as the story progresses suki chimas  masks of indifference start slipping  just a little at a time until we see him  for what he truly is an intelligent  thinking player capable of turning any  match around with a love for the game  that's second to none that a character  seemingly on the sidelines suddenly  turns into the hero of season threes  both unexpected and sweetly satisfying  that's what high Q does best it  surprises you in little doses and that's  all it takes to make its characters  endearing and memorable  while on the subject of characters can  be remiss not to talk about high Q's art  style its hurry to food or Dante's  illustrations that make the characters  pop with life and grab your attention  to a great extent the focus is on the  facial features distinct eyes pointy  chins a lot of crazy hair all drawn with  sharp pencil lines if perhaps Peaks a  fuller Dante's admiration of bleach  created tight Deku bow another artist  with a drawing style all his own and  very different from others what you'll  get are characters with faces that  express a myriad of emotions with his  cast of teenage boys the artist could be  forgiven for getting a little lazy here  and there but that doesn't happen even  the minor characters have been drawn  with some amount of detail you  definitely won't find same face syndrome  here the mangaka also has fun with the  illustrations take the case of bokuto  Kotaro the silver head Ace of bucura  Donny high school's volleyball team who  strangely resembles a great horned owl  why is this relevant you asked because  foo Kuro in Japanese means owl you get  that then there's the way the mangaka  draws karma with an exaggerated crown  and cloak each time someone calls him  king of the court a hated nickname from  middle school that's a testament not to  the players caliber for his image as an  unapproachable monarch who terrorized  his teammates  the arts at its best in the action  sequences as is with most sports or  battle anime fluid lines and beautiful  proportions bring the players and the  game to life as a player stretches his  body to its limits to make an impossible  save the artwork heat right up in some  sequences like when a fearsome rival  delivers a punishing smackdown the  drawing is more manga light to make the  moment more impactful we've seen the  quality of the art dropping quite a few  anime but this hasn't happened in high Q  hopefully it never will if clever  character design and a strong art style  do a lot of the talking in high Q the  storytelling does the rest PI Q is at  its core a simple tale of a bunch of  high schoolers working towards their  dreams of volleyball greatness it could  mean beating stronger teams to get to  the Nationals it could even be a more  personal goal like besting arrival you  look up to in the game there are no  grand and lofty ideas yet it's in its  simplicity that high Q shines its  brightest  rather than taking the viewer on an  emotional roller coaster  the mangaka uses small moments that  leave nuts impressions one such moment  is the fight between Hinata and Kageyama  that comes after the spike it expresses  its wish to try out a new form of attack  with barely any time left for the next  tournament it's not unusual to see the  two squabble they're always at each  other's throats but you know this is the  first disagreement that might just break  their fragile partnership the writer  cleverly uses a bystander with the timid  Agha cheeky toka to drive this point  home as the quarrel intensifies joshi  begs the two boys to be friends again  but the viewer is the only one listening  to her please  one of my favorite pieces of writing in  high q-- is the silent dinner at the end  of the first season karasuno have just  lost a big match after coming within  touching distance of victory sitting  around a table full of food  the boys dig in without a sound eating  like there's no tomorrow  you've seen them eat a hearty meal after  a hard-fought game many times before but  this time as their tears flow freely in  between mouthfuls of food and their  muffled sob speak volumes you can't help  but feel their heartbreak and share  their sorrow between these smartly  written scenes the story progresses at a  brisk pace with few dull moments yet it  also knows when to take its time around  certain situations the perfect example  of this is in season 3 where a single  match stretches into all 10 episodes but  never once feels like the drag the  writing doesn't falter even in the  lighter moments keeping the momentum  going with humor and fun banter there  are tiny gems of humor in the most  unexpected places - such as the Zoe's ng  high school teams banner that sports the  slogan simplicity and fortitude  that's wrong with that except that the  boys are Zoe's ng are among the  flashiest of most free-spirited with a  playing style to match these little  tricks and treats are what make hike use  writing worthy of praise  if you've read the Haiku manga you've  seen the mangakas message on page two of  volume one crude are they says the idea  behind high q-- is to create a story  with fun and great characters  we'll have you rooting for them even if  you don't know a thing about volleyball  when I say mission accomplished high q--  sucks you into a story and makes you  stay invested in his characters there  are big expectations of season 4 is  Hinata and the boys head to Tokyo for  the Nationals  it's my number one pick for anime shows  coming to us in January you can check  out my video of the top 10 most  anticipated winter 2020 anime on this  channel and that's it for today's video  I hope you're thinking about watching  haikyuu if you haven't already drop me a  like if you enjoyed this video and  subscribe to Vinnie tube for more anime  content I'll see you again soon  [Music]
 

